  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a combination of global and local search strategies to minimize the maximum overlap integral (C) for a step function h on [0,2] with integral 1. As it is a non-convex optimization problem with hard constraints, we leverage multi-resolution exploration, adaptive local search, and stochastic restarts. By testing different grid resolutions (n_points) and combining local optimization with global random restarts, we balance exploration and exploitation to find a configuration that reduces C below 0.3808 while satisfying h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and \u03a3h[i] = n/2 for the grid resolution.\n\nMathematical specification\n- Representation: h is a vector of length n_points (n) with dx = 2/n\n- Integral constraint: \u03a3h[i] = n/2 (after projection)\n- Optimization objective: Minimize C = max_k (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx\n- Each candidate must satisfy: \n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n  - \u03a3h[i] = n/2\n- The solution vector is validated by project_feasible and compared using compute_c5 with the original correlation method\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Load parent solution if available, else start from default h\n2. Project initial_h_values to local feasible form\n3. Phase 1 (local refinement):\n   - Apply exact_cd(h, n) for 300 adjustments\n   - Run slsqp_opt with \u03b1 grid [10000, 50000]\n4. Phase 2 (multi-resolution):\n   - Test n_points \u2208 [400, 500, 800, 900, 1000]\n   - For each n: interpolate from current best, run exact_cd then SLSQP\n5. Phase 3 (global restarts):\n   - If time > 500s: restart from n \u2208 [200, 300, 400, 500, 700] with random patterns\n6. Phase 4 (local polish):\n   - Finish with exact_cd for 100 more steps on best candidate\n7. At any phase: if timeout, store best solution and return immediately\n\nSearch choices and variations\n- Primary hyperparameters: \n  - n_list = [400, 500, 800, 900, 1000]\n  - SLSQP \u03b1 schedules: [10000, 50000] then [1000, 5000]\n  - Multi-resolution shift: \u00b1100 to parent's n\n  - Global restart hyperparameter: \u03b1=0.995 (initial)\n  - Random candidate generation: uniform [0,1] then project_feasible\n- Adaptive cooling: \n  - Local search: fixed 100 steps for warm restarts\n  - Global cooling factor: =0.995 applied if time exceeds deadline - 500\n\nCompute budget and robustness\n- Time management: Monitor elapsed time at beginning of each phase\n- Outlet checks: Compute deadline at start and check every 5s\n- Fallback handling: Preserve best solution from phase 1 after every 10 evaluation failures\n- Temperature scaling: Global restarts use [50, 100] seconds for 5 candidate generations\n- C5 validation: Each candidate's C is recomputed with the full correlation method\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Return only (h, c5, n_points) for best_c5 found\n2. Each candidate: \n   - Units: h[i] \u2208 R\n   - Integral: \u03a3h[i] == n/2 (exactly, within 1e-12)\n   - Bounds: min(h) \u2265 -0 (clip to 0) max(h) \u2264 +1 (clip to 1)\n3. C5 computation: \n   - c5 = compute_c5(h, n_points) = max correlation * dx\n4. Termination criteria:\n   - No improvement over last 20 candidates\n   - Timeout reached before any meaningful improvement\n   - Better than 0.3808 must be rigorously verified across multiple restarts\n\nhelper_servers:\n   project_feasible(h, n): Axiom: projects to [0,1] and adjusts integral\n   compute_c5(h, n): Axiom: computes C5 using mode='full' correlation\n   interpolate_h(h_orig, n_orig, n_new): Axiom: bilinear interpolation\n   random_candidate(n): Axiom: uniform random in [0,1] space\n\nedge_cases:\n   No parent available: handled by default h = np.full(500, 0.5)\n   n_points even/odd: handled by interpolation\n   Invalid candidate: any vector from clamping/subgradient\n\nThe plan ensures a complete utilization of the available time while providing a fallback mechanism if the algorithm fails to find improvement.",
